      "latency_ms": 1812.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 775,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, enabling chemical reactions in biological systems.<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, allowing insects to walk on water and plants to transport water upward (capillary action).<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in cold climates. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 361 million km²). Found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere. |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, DNA replication, etc., all occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Transport** – blood, sap, and cellular fluids move nutrients, waste, and signals.<br>• **Structural** – cells are ~70 % water; water maintains turgor pressure in plants and provides shape to many organisms. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking, cooking, sanitation.<br>• Agriculture (irrigation).<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines).<br>• Manufacturing (cooling, solvents, chemical synthesis).<br>• Recreation (swimming, sports). |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Regulates Earth’s temperature via the water cycle (evaporation → condensation → precipitation).<br>• Supports ecosystems (aquatic habitats, wetlands).<br>• Influences weather patterns and climate through latent heat exchange. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n- **Polarity** gives it the ability to form hydrogen bonds, which are responsible for most of its unusual physical properties (high boiling point, surface tension, etc.).\n- **Hydrogen bonding** creates a dynamic network that constantly breaks and reforms, allowing water to flow easily while still holding together as a liquid.\n- **Self‑ionization** (2 H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + OH⁻) provides a very weak but crucial source of acidity and basicity, enabling acid–base chemistry in living systems.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) whose ability to form hydrogen bonds gives it a suite of remarkable physical and chemical properties. These properties make it an unparalleled solvent, a regulator of temperature, a medium for life’s chemistry, and a cornerstone of Earth’s climate and ecosystems."
